1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
 Hey everyone, Ryan here!

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:07,480
 This video is on a topic that many of you have been asking for.

3
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:14,440
 So some exciting things were happening at the Euro-Period9 conference in June of 2018.

4
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:21,880
 The American Academy of Peridontology, or the AAP, officially announced a new Peridontal

5
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:23,880
 classification system.

6
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:29,200
 And the guidelines had previously not been updated since 1999.

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,320
 So this is a pretty big deal.

8
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:37,000
 Additionally, the Integrated National Board dental exam is exclusively using this new

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:38,980
 classification system.

10
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:44,280
 So I'm here to cover it for you and help you feel comfortable using it to diagnose

11
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:47,080
 patient cases.

12
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:51,960
 So let's look at a brief history of Peridontal classifications.

13
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:58,680
 In the mid-1970s, we started with chronic marginal versus juvenile periodontitis.

14
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:05,040
 In the mid-1980s, that's changed to adult and juvenile periodontitis.

15
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:10,240
 And then shortly after, they added in more specific categories, like Type N through

16
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:11,240
 Type V.

17
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:17,040
 In the late 1990s, they introduced chronic and aggressive periodontitis, which we've

18
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:24,440
 used for roughly two decades, and then announced in 2017 and implemented in 2018, the staging

19
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:29,600
 and grading system is the newest one we have, and the one we'll spend the most time on in

20
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:31,720
 this video.

21
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:39,360
 So the major changes implemented at this conference is that there are now four main categories

22
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:41,480
 of disease processes.

23
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:47,800
 These are periodontal health and gingival diseases and conditions, periodontitis, period

24
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:54,760
 implant diseases and conditions, and periodontal manifestation of systemic diseases and developmental

25
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:56,400
 and acquired conditions.

26
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:04,040
 Now we're going to focus on the first three in this video in that order.

27
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,800
 So we start with periodontal health.

28
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:12,960
 Now if things get worse, that turns to gingivitis, and if things get worse still, that turns to

29
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:14,520
 periodontitis.

30
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:20,080
 Now periodontal health and gingival diseases only includes these first two, so let's focus

31
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:22,920
 on those to start with.

32
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:29,000
 The case definition of periodontal health is healthy, soft, and hard tissue surrounding

33
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,760
 the teeth.

34
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:36,000
 That being said, some amount of inflammation is acceptable because let's face it, no one's

35
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 perfect.

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:44,480
 So localized, minimal, and delayed bleeding on probing at isolated sites is hard to avoid

37
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:49,440
 and may fall within the spectrum of clinical health.

38
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:55,180
 Now that could be minimal bleeding on probing at less than 10% of sites and probing depths

39
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:57,480
 at three millimeters or below.

40
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:03,520
 These things describe maybe one or two sites with some evidence of clinical inflammation,

41
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:08,120
 but generally overall this is a situation of health.

42
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:13,680
 Now this can happen on either an intact or a reduced periodontum, so let's define those

43
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:14,960
 real quick.

44
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:21,560
 An intact periodontum is one with no loss of periodontal tissue.

45
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:29,640
 A reduced periodontum is a periodontum with pre-existing loss of periodontal tissue,

46
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:39,440
 but and this is important, is not currently undergoing loss of bone or connective tissue.

47
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:45,680
 So reduced periodontum could be the result of hard toothbrush scrubbing leading to gingival

48
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:50,640
 recession or crown lengthening performed by a dentist.

49
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:56,040
 In these cases the patient could have no periodontitis history at all.

50
00:03:56,040 --> 00:04:00,680
 These are things that were involving toothbrush scrubbing and crown lengthening performed by

51
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:01,680
 the dentist.

52
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:06,000
 So that patient may have a reduced periodontum and they never had periodontal disease at

53
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,080
 all.

54
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:13,300
 In this case, those types of patients that fall in this category have no increased risk

55
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:15,600
 of periodontitis.

56
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:22,420
 But reduced periodontum could also be the result of previous periodontitis that caused

57
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:27,920
 the clinical attachment loss, but now the condition is currently stable because we're

58
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:31,000
 in a situation of periodontal health.

59
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,440
 By the way, periodontal stability is characterized by successful treatment through control of

60
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:42,720
 local and systemic risk factors, resulting in bleeding on probing at less than 10% of

61
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:51,320
 sites and probing depths at 3mm or below, and there's no progressive periodontal destruction.

62
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:58,040
 So reduced periodontum, intact periodontum, both of those situations could be the result

63
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:05,960
 of, or could be, included in this realm of periodontal health.

64
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:11,640
 Now the case definition of gingivitis is clinical evidence of inflammation.

65
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:18,480
 And this involves things like erythema, redness, edema, this is localized swelling, and bleeding

66
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:19,560
 on probing.

67
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:25,440
 So now we have bleeding on probing at greater than or equal to 10% of sites.

68
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:31,160
 But those probing depths are still pretty controlled at 3mm or below.

69
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:37,280
 Stable periodontum, again, we're not seeing the progressive loss of alveolar bone and

70
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:39,600
 clinical attachment.

71
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:44,100
 This is defined as gingivitis.

72
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:49,760
 We're mostly focusing on the big change here is this part right here.

73
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:54,200
 Bleeding on probing and these clinical signs of inflammation that are occurring at more

74
00:05:54,200 --> 00:06:00,600
 than just one or two sites, now it's either localized to the anterior region of the mouth

75
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:05,680
 or the posterior region of the mouth, or it's generalized to the entire mouth.

76
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:10,800
 Okay, so now let's move to periodontitis.

77
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:16,000
 So periodontitis is defined as the loss of periodontal tissue support due to microbially

78
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,200
 associated host-mediated inflammation.

79
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:24,080
 Now we're talking about deeper probing depths and we're also talking about the presence

80
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:26,920
 of interproximal attachment loss.

81
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:30,460
 So much of this is the same that we've known before.

82
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:33,520
 Without plaque, there is no disease.

83
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:40,480
 Plaque induces an inflammatory response and the body runs away as the bone and soft tissue

84
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:44,000
 attachment retreats away from that plaque.

85
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,120
 It's evaluated through the clinical attachment or the clinical evaluation, I should say, of

86
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:54,520
 attachment levels or the radiographic assessment of bone levels.

87
00:06:54,520 --> 00:07:01,920
 And the key here is interproximal, that's an important word in this definition.

88
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:06,560
 So we're not talking so much about straight facial and straight lingual.

89
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:12,440
 We're focusing more on the attachment loss in the mesial and distal areas between the

90
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:14,520
 teeth.

91
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:22,880
 Okay, so for periodontitis classification, the new additions are staging and grading.

92
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:25,240
 So let's look at staging first.

93
00:07:25,240 --> 00:07:33,560
 The stage is determined by the severity and the extent of disease at presentation.

94
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:36,200
 So this is a static measurement.

95
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:40,440
 At this moment in time, what is that patient's presentation?

96
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:47,360
 Now we'll look at things like severity, complexity, and extent and distribution and how all three

97
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:51,760
 of these work together to determine your stage.

98
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:57,160
 Now staging intends to classify the patient's disease based on the measurable amount of

99
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:04,600
 destroyed and/or damaged tissue as the result of periodontitis and to assess the specific

100
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:11,040
 factors that may attribute to the complexity of long-term case management.

101
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:19,040
 Staging is measured from one to four, that's from slight to severe.

102
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:25,820
 So the first thing you look at to determine staging is severity and severity is primarily

103
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:32,120
 based on interdental clinical attachment loss or CAL.

104
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:38,640
 CAL is measured from the CEJ, that's the Cemento enamel junction, to the base of the pocket.

105
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:44,880
 And your worst interproximal CAL, that's the biggest number, the greatest loss of attachment

106
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:50,000
 you have in the mouth, gives you your baseline stage.

107
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,240
 And again, this is not straight facial or straight lingual.

108
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:57,040
 We're talking about the areas in between the teeth.

109
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:03,240
 So if that worst interdental CAL is one or two millimeters, that defines a stage one,

110
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:06,120
 three or four, that's a stage two.

111
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:12,720
 If it's five or more, then we're automatically in stage three or stage four territory.

112
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:19,160
 If clinical attachment loss is not available, radiographic bone loss can be used as the

113
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:21,560
 proxy instead.

114
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:27,200
 And tooth loss, due to peridontitis, can shift the stage to a higher level.

115
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:31,160
 If that patient's loss to a certain amount of teeth, we could bump up a stage one or

116
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:34,120
 stage two to a stage three or stage four.

117
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:41,720
 And we'll talk more about those specifics when we get to specific examples.

118
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:47,800
 Complexity is primarily based on local factors, like probing depth, the nature of the bone

119
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:53,240
 loss of its horizontal or vertical, if there's forcation involvement, if there's occlusal

120
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:57,680
 trauma and bike collapse, just to name a few.

121
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:03,880
 And one or more of these complexity factors can shift the stage to a higher level.

122
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:09,920
 For example, if your largest probing pocket depth is five millimeters, it can upgrade

123
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:17,160
 a stage one to a stage two, or if you have probing pocket depth above six millimeters

124
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:21,760
 with forcation involvement, you can upgrade that to a stage three.

125
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:26,440
 And if you have secondary occlusal trauma and bike collapse, you could maybe even upgrade

126
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:28,240
 that patient to a stage four.

127
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:32,720
 So that's how complexity factors can work.

128
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:38,440
 There are also some special factors to consider if you have vertical bone loss at three millimeters

129
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:44,400
 or more, that puts them at an automatic stage three or four, forcation involvement, class

130
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:49,840
 two or class three, class two means you have a horizontal forcation that's greater than

131
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:55,400
 three millimeters, class three means that you have a through and through forcation involvement

132
00:10:55,400 --> 00:11:01,160
 so you can pass that neighbors probe all the way through the forcation area.

133
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:04,680
 That's an automatic stage three or four as well.

134
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:13,020
 And if the patient has less than 20 teeth remaining, they're an automatic stage four.

135
00:11:13,020 --> 00:11:17,080
 And the last thing that goes into staging is extent and distribution.

136
00:11:17,080 --> 00:11:25,160
 And this is based on the percentage of teeth that are involved in that severity or complexity.

137
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:31,560
 So if the patient has a localized extent and distribution, that means that less than 30

138
00:11:31,560 --> 00:11:38,600
 percent of their teeth are involved in that severity or complexity, generalized means

139
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:43,920
 that 30 percent or more of their teeth are involved at that severity.

140
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:49,880
 And a molar incisor pattern is a very specific pattern where only incisors and molars are

141
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:50,880
 included.

142
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:56,200
 And this is kind of our classic localized aggressive periodontitis.

143
00:11:56,200 --> 00:12:03,560
 So to put this in a little bit more relevant perspective, if a patient has 32 teeth, that's

144
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:11,160
 all their teeth, including their wisdom teeth, then this 30 percent threshold is 10 teeth.

145
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:16,160
 So the cutoff for becoming generalized is 10 teeth being affected.

146
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:21,520
 If the patient had their wisdom teeth removed and they have 28 teeth, then this 30 percent

147
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:29,340
 threshold is at 8 teeth, so the cutoff for becoming generalized would be if 8 teeth were

148
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:31,280
 affected.

149
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:37,560
 And again, if molars and incisors are exclusively involved, we instead, we don't call it localized,

150
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:42,360
 we call it a molar incisor pattern to be even more specific.

151
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:49,200
 So since there's no longer aggressive periodontitis, that's no longer a thing, after this new classification

152
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:52,480
 system was announced, we call it molar incisor.

153
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:58,240
 So you take one of these three and add it to the stage as a descriptor, so you could

154
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:07,160
 call it localized stage three, or molar incisor stage four, for example.

155
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:14,040
 So to put it all together, you start with the most severe interdental cal and select the

156
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:16,120
 appropriate stage.

157
00:13:16,120 --> 00:13:21,480
 If cal is not available, you do the same thing with radiographic bone loss.

158
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:25,000
 Then you look at the teeth lost due to periodontitis.

159
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,200
 If four or less, then you upgrade to stage three.

160
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:32,480
 If five or more, you upgrade to stage four.

161
00:13:32,480 --> 00:13:35,680
 And then you look at the local complexity factors.

162
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:40,240
 So if there are factors that are appropriate to a patient like vertical bone loss greater

163
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:46,360
 than three millimeters, or less than 20 remaining teeth, you would upgrade to the appropriate

164
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:47,840
 stage.

165
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:51,000
 Stage one is slight severity.

166
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:52,760
 Stage two is moderate.

167
00:13:52,760 --> 00:13:55,760
 Stage three and stage four are severe.

168
00:13:55,760 --> 00:14:04,880
 And remember, there can only be one stage per patient, so use the most severe one.

169
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:07,080
 All right now, let's talk about the grade.

170
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:12,640
 So the grade is determined by the rate of disease progression, the responsiveness to

171
00:14:12,640 --> 00:14:17,000
 therapy, and their overall assessment of risk.

172
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:22,920
 So this is based on longitudinal observation, the assessment of bone loss over time, and

173
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:26,080
 other risk factors like smoking and diabetes.

174
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:32,760
 So grading is a dynamic measurement, how things are changing over time.

175
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:38,960
 And it aims to indicate the rate of periodontitis progression, the responsiveness to their therapy,

176
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:43,440
 and potential impact on their systemic health.

177
00:14:43,440 --> 00:14:51,240
 So this one is measured from A to C, from slow to rapid.

178
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:54,320
 Now thankfully, grading is a bit more straightforward.

179
00:14:54,320 --> 00:15:00,860
 And clinicians should initially assume a grade of B, and then look for specific evidence

180
00:15:00,860 --> 00:15:07,840
 to shift to either grade A or grade C. And just like in staging, the first row here is

181
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:09,680
 the most important.

182
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:18,760
 So we look at how an interdental cal or radiographic bone loss is changing over a period of five

183
00:15:18,760 --> 00:15:21,920
 years.

184
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:28,840
 And so if it's more loss of attachment or bone loss or bone levels over this period of

185
00:15:28,840 --> 00:15:32,680
 five years, we're favoring more towards a rapid disease progression.

186
00:15:32,680 --> 00:15:37,640
 If there's not as much loss, we're more towards a slow rate of progression.

187
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:44,480
 Secondarily, we can look at percent of radiographic bone loss compared to age.

188
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:52,120
 So this would be like if you had a 50% bone loss for a 50-year-old patient.

189
00:15:52,120 --> 00:15:59,680
 That would be a ratio of 50-50, which would be 1, and a ratio of 1 would put you in grade

190
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:07,920
 B. Now if you had 50% bone loss for a 25-year-old, that would be a ratio of 2, and put you at

191
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:09,920
 grade C.

192
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:14,440
 The case phenotype is talking about if the amount of plaque you see is consistent with

193
00:16:14,440 --> 00:16:16,320
 the level of disease.

194
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:23,000
 So heavy biofilm deposits with low levels of destruction would be a grade A situation.

195
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:28,020
 If the destruction is consistent with the biofilm deposits, that would be grade B.

196
00:16:28,020 --> 00:16:35,000
 And if the destruction you see exceeds the expectations given those biofilm deposits,

197
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:40,900
 that's suggestive of periods of rapid progression and/or early onset disease that would put

198
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:44,240
 you at a grade C situation.

199
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:49,280
 For smoking the more smoking you do, the more uncontrolled your diabetes is, those things

200
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:53,000
 are going to make the disease progress more rapidly.

201
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:00,820
 And CRP stands for C Reactive Protein, an elevated serum, C Reactive Protein, is a systemic

202
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:06,920
 marker of inflammation and a documented risk factor for cardiovascular disease and otherwise

203
00:17:06,920 --> 00:17:08,120
 healthy people.

204
00:17:08,120 --> 00:17:14,320
 So the more CRP you have, means you're at higher risk of a faster progressing disease.

205
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:20,880
 So grade A is slow, grade B is moderate, grade C is rapid.

206
00:17:20,880 --> 00:17:26,440
 And just like with staging, you only give one grade per patient.

207
00:17:26,440 --> 00:17:33,200
 But since it's dynamic, the grade might change over time as risk factors are modified, and

208
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:39,280
 the idea is to predict future progression, based on past progression, in the presence

209
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:44,680
 of these risk factors.

210
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:51,840
 So some other considerations, a peridontitis patient is a peridontitis patient for life,

211
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:57,520
 and patients should not return to the level of evaluation and maintenance identical to

212
00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:00,840
 a patient who has never had peridontitis.

213
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:07,360
 So for someone who hasn't had peridontitis, they get an annual or twice a year exam and

214
00:18:07,360 --> 00:18:08,520
 cleaning.

215
00:18:08,520 --> 00:18:14,880
 For a patient who has peridontitis, they should always be treated with a certain level of scrutiny

216
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:22,280
 and maintenance in order that they don't relapse into a stage of progressive bone loss and

217
00:18:22,280 --> 00:18:25,160
 connective tissue loss.

218
00:18:25,160 --> 00:18:33,160
 And despite substantial research on aggressive peridontitis since that last 1999 workshop,

219
00:18:33,160 --> 00:18:38,400
 there's currently insufficient evidence to consider aggressive and chronic peridontitis

220
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:42,280
 as to pathophysiologically distinct diseases.

221
00:18:42,280 --> 00:18:48,680
 So that distinction is no more.

222
00:18:48,680 --> 00:18:51,920
 Alright so let's have some fun and go over some cases.

223
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:57,040
 Now I designed this patient box to mimic what you'll see on the integrated board exam.

224
00:18:57,040 --> 00:19:00,960
 So this could be part of a question prompt that you get.

225
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:06,480
 So for this case number one, we have a male who's 50 years old.

226
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:11,160
 Their chief complaint is I'm concerned about some of my front teeth that drift around.

227
00:19:11,160 --> 00:19:13,920
 They smoke a half pack of cigarettes every day.

228
00:19:13,920 --> 00:19:20,760
 They have minimal biofilm deposits, interdental cow that ranges from 2 to 8 millimeters.

229
00:19:20,760 --> 00:19:27,600
 The 14 teeth have interdental cow that's greater than or equal to 5 millimeters.

230
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:32,840
